</script>

Adverse selection is an economic problem that arises whenever one party to a transaction knows something the other party does not, and exploits that informational advantage. The term originates in insurance markets: people who are most likely to file claims are also the most motivated to buy insurance, so an insurer that prices its policies on the average risk of the population will attract a pool of customers that is riskier than average. The insurer has been _adversely selected_. It attracted the wrong customers because it could not tell the good risks from the bad ones.

The problem appears throughout finance, but it is most central to [market making](../market-making/), on which this document builds. A market maker continuously quotes a bid, the price at which it will buy a security, and an ask, the price at which it will sell. Every time it does so, it faces the possibility that the person on the other side of the trade knows something it does not. Suppose a market maker quotes a bid of $\$50.00$ and an ask of $\$50.05$ on Acme Corp stock. A hedge fund that has spent weeks analyzing Acme's supply chain discovers that a key supplier has gone bankrupt, information the market has not yet priced in. The hedge fund sells 100,000 shares to the market maker at $\$50.00$. When the news breaks and Acme drops to $\$47$, the market maker has lost $\$3$ per share, or $\$300{,}000$, on a trade it would never have made at that price had it known what the hedge fund knew.

# The spread as compensation

This risk is the primary reason market makers charge a bid-ask spread. The spread is not simply a fee for service. It is compensation for the statistical certainty that some fraction of the market maker's counterparties are better informed. The market maker loses on informed trades and earns the spread on uninformed ones, and it is profitable overall when the second outweighs the first. The difficulty is that it cannot tell, at the moment of a trade, which kind of counterparty it faces. It can only set its spread according to the adverse selection it expects on average across all trades.

A model due to Glosten and Milgrom [](cite:glosten1985) makes the argument exact in its simplest case. Suppose a stock will turn out to be worth either a high value $V_H$ or a low value $V_L$, each with probability one half, so that its expected value is $\bar{V} = (V_H + V_L)/2$. A fraction $\pi$ of the traders who arrive are informed: they know the value, and buy if it is high and sell if it is low. The rest are uninformed and buy or sell with equal probability. The market maker cannot tell the two kinds apart, but it can reason about what an order reveals. A buy order arrives with probability $\pi + (1 - \pi)/2$ when the value is high and with probability $(1 - \pi)/2$ when it is low, so by Bayes' rule the probability that the value is high, given that someone wants to buy, is $(1 + \pi)/2$. A market maker that expects to break even on each trade therefore sets its ask at the expected value of the stock given a buy order, and its bid likewise given a sell order:

$$
	\text{ask} = \bar{V} + \frac{\pi}{2}(V_H - V_L), \qquad \text{bid} = \bar{V} - \frac{\pi}{2}(V_H - V_L). @tag(quotes)
$$

The spread is the difference of the two,

$$
	\text{ask} - \text{bid} = \pi\,(V_H - V_L). @tag(spread)
$$

<Quotes />

Two things widen the spread in [](eq:spread): a larger share $\pi$ of informed traders, and a larger gap $V_H - V_L$ between what the informed might know and what the public believes. For the Acme example, with the stock worth $\$53$ or $\$47$, a market maker facing one informed trader in a hundred breaks even with a spread of $0.01 \times 6 = \$0.06$, while one facing one informed trader in ten needs $\$0.60$. With no informed traders the spread vanishes, since an order then reveals nothing. The model leaves out the market maker's other costs and risks, which add to the spread in practice, but it isolates the part that is owed to information alone.

# Retail and institutional order flow

This logic explains why market makers care intensely about the _type_ of order flow they receive. Retail customers, individual investors trading from personal accounts, are generally uninformed in the sense of the model. They trade on widely available news, personal financial needs, or general sentiment, not on proprietary research, faster data feeds, or sophisticated quantitative models. A market maker that buys shares from a retail customer who is selling to rebalance a retirement portfolio faces very little risk that the customer knows something about the stock's near-term direction. Retail order flow is therefore the most profitable kind of flow: the market maker earns the spread on nearly every trade with minimal losses to adverse selection. This is why market makers pay brokerages a premium, through [payment for order flow](../payment-for-order-flow/), to receive retail orders.

Institutional order flow carries much higher adverse selection risk. Hedge funds, proprietary trading firms, and quantitative desks often possess better information, faster execution infrastructure, or more sophisticated analytical tools. A market maker trading against such a counterparty faces a meaningful probability of being on the wrong side of an informed bet. For this reason, market makers quote wider spreads to institutional counterparties, or handle their orders through different channels with less favorable pricing.

# Spreads across instruments

Adverse selection also explains why spreads vary across instruments. A highly liquid large-cap stock like Apple has abundant public information, fast price discovery, and many competing market makers, all of which reduce the information any single counterparty is likely to hold. Both $\pi$ and $V_H - V_L$ are small, and the spread is a penny or two. A small-cap biotech stock awaiting a regulatory decision on its only drug, by contrast, has enormous information asymmetry: someone may know the trial results before they are public, and the two possible values of the stock are far apart. The adverse selection risk is high, and market makers widen their spreads to compensate.
